-- games/platinum.lua — Pokémon Platinum (US, Rev 1 · ROM code CPUE).
--
-- Loaded by ../main.lua, which reads the ROM header and hands this file the game name as its
-- chunk argument. Open main.lua in BizHawk's Lua console; never open this file directly.
--
-- ⭐⭐ WHAT MAKES GEN IV DIFFERENT FROM GEN V: WE HAVE THE SOURCE.
-- research/re/pokeplatinum is pret/pokeplatinum, a MATCHING decompilation, and Ola's cartridge is
-- byte-for-byte what it builds (SHA-1 0862ec35…755c). The build was reproduced here, so its 46,407
-- symbols are REAL addresses in his game (research/re/pt_symbols.txt). Finding an anchor is a grep,
-- not a Ghidra session. Every address and offset below therefore cites the decomp file and line it
-- came from, AND the retail-ROM instruction that proves it — because a struct definition is a lead,
-- not a trace (CLAUDE.md Rule 5), and the compiler is free to lay a struct out its own way.
--
-- ⛔ ONE ROOT TABLE, FROM LINE ONE (Ola's condition for doing Platinum first). Every ABSOLUTE
-- address lives in `A` below and nowhere else. Struct OFFSETS are portable and live with the code
-- that uses them. That division is CLAUDE.md Rule 4, and it is what will make Diamond/Pearl a
-- root-remap pass instead of a second arc — Platinum is a REBUILD of D/P, not a sibling (122
-- overlays vs 87, zero same-numbered load addresses agreeing), so there is no delta model to find
-- and the only thing that ports is exactly this: the offsets.
--
-- WHAT IS IN HERE SO FAR, one MODULE each, in the order they are registered — which is also their
-- PRIORITY. ⚠ KEEP THIS LIST HONEST: it is the file's only statement of which reader outranks which,
-- and a stale one will misdirect the next person to add a screen (it already did once).
--   1. the boot sequence — the legal card, GAME FREAK, the wordmark: the only words between
--      power-on and the title, and every one of them a picture;
--   2. the title screen — the version logo, the developer line and PRESS START;
--   3. the main menu — Continue / New Game and the rest, plus the saved game's own summary;
--   3b. Mystery Gift and the Wonder Card album — two applications in the main-menu overlay;
--   4. the naming keyboard — the prompt, the page, the key under the cursor and the name so far;
--   5. the save screen — the information panel and the Yes/No buttons;
--   6. field dialogue and signposts — page by page, in the game's own words, honouring the page
--      breaks the player presses A through;
--   7. the opening — Professor Rowan's speech, the info screens, the choice boxes (including the
--      rival's name), and the boy/girl selection, which has no text of its own at all;
--   8. the TV report — the Prof. Rowan comment that closes the opening, a child of module 7;
--   9. the start menu — the X menu, whose rows include the player's own name.
--   9b. the ending — the Hall of Fame, the automatic save after it and the credits, each
--      gated on its own application or field task;
--  10. overworld navigation and route following, including ledges and slopes;
--  11. starter selection;
--  12–13. battle command and move menus;
--  14–18. field party, bag, summary, Pokédex and Berry Tags;
--  19–20. battle targets/choices and battle party/bag;
--  21–23. shops, Options and Trainer Card (including the badge case).
-- ⛔ TWO ORDERINGS ARE LOAD-BEARING. 4 before 7: the keyboard is a CHILD of the opening, so the
-- opening's application is still the current one while it is up (the opening also stands down on its
-- own account, but the order is the belt to that pair of braces). And 5 before 6: the message box
-- stays open for the whole save flow, so the dialogue reader would otherwise take every frame of it
-- and the buttons would never get one.
-- Battle messages, status keys and level-up panels run before module dispatch. Field child apps
-- exclude field dialogue and navigation through field_map_live; they do not depend on priority.
--
-- ⏱ ⛔⛔ NOTHING IN THIS FILE MAY COUNT THE GAME'S FRAMES, AND NOTHING DOES. Every gate below keys
-- on a STATE the engine sets, never on a duration — which is what makes the readers survive a ROM
-- HACK that changes the frame limit (Ola, 2026-09-05: "I do want to make it work with hacks too").
--
-- The rate is worth knowing because it is easy to misread, and because the comments quote it: on
-- RETAIL the application logic ticks at about 30 Hz, not 60. NitroMain zeroes gSystem.frameCounter
-- at the bottom of its loop and the VBlank interrupt increments it, so after RunApplication returns
-- the loop asks `if (!gSystem.frameCounter)` — "did the app finish inside one frame?" — and when it
-- did, waits for a VBlank there AND again at the bottom. A fast frame therefore costs TWO VBlanks,
-- i.e. a 30 Hz governor on the application logic (src/main.c:120-152), while the rendering stays at
-- 60. The game's own code says so in the clearest possible way: the TV report writes its delays as
-- `2 * 30` and `3 * 30`.
--
-- ⭐ SO EVERY "N SECONDS" IN THE COMMENTS BELOW IS A RETAIL FIGURE, and the common 60 fps hacks —
-- which remove that inner wait — halve every one of them. That costs the comments their precision
-- and costs the READERS nothing, which is the whole point of gating on state. ⛔ If a future reader
-- ever does need a duration, read the game's own countdown and watch it reach zero; do NOT convert
-- ticks to seconds against a constant, because that constant is exactly what a hack changes. There
-- is no frames-per-second value anywhere in RAM to read instead — PlayTime runs off a hardware
-- timer, so the rate is emergent from the shape of that loop and cannot be asked for.
-- ⚠ OUR Lua loop is a different clock: once per emu.frameadvance(), i.e. 60 Hz whatever the game
-- does. A counter WE keep (the keyboard's prompt retry) is in 60ths; a counter the GAME keeps is in
-- its own ticks. Caught by codex-review 2026-09-05, which is also why "fifteen seconds" is thirty.
--
-- ⭐ 2 AND 3 ARE SEPARATE APPLICATIONS, and that is the shape of the whole game. Gen IV has no
-- single "current screen" — each one is an application with its own callbacks, its own data and
-- often its own overlay, so a reader is gated by WHICH APPLICATION IS RUNNING (app_data below) and
-- then by that screen's own state. A reader written for the field will never see the intro; that is
-- not a bug in the gate, it is what the gate is for.

-- From the loader: the game name, and core(), which loads scripts/core/<name>.lua — the parts that
-- are the same for EVERY game (Ola, 2026-09-04). Neither is ever nil.
local GAME, core = ...
-- ES: la ventana de la consola de Lua se hace lenta al acumular texto, y todo lo hablado se escribe en ella.
-- Se vacia cada 300 lineas para que los tirones de sonido no aparezcan con el paso del tiempo.
do
	local write, clear, lines = console.writeline, console.clear, 0
	if write and clear then
		console.writeline = function(...)
			lines = lines + 1
			if lines > 300 then lines = 0; clear() end
			return write(...)
		end
	end
end

------------------------------------------------------------------ CONFIG -----
-- FAST_TEXT: keep the game's typewriter at its fastest, whatever the player's TEXT SPEED option
-- says, by clearing the delay on every live text printer each frame.
--
-- ⭐ THAT IS ALL THAT IS NEEDED, and proving it took a detour worth recording. Ola asked for Black
-- and White's instant text so that A would work the moment he had heard a page. Gen IV cannot do
-- page-at-once by writing data — the renderer draws one glyph per call — so a 100-byte ARM9 CODE
-- patch was built, tested, and WORKED. He then removed it himself (2026-09-05): "the a press is not
-- needed here, you can just set the speed option to fast, so it's fine, it's no game modifying."
--
-- He is right, and the reason is this file's own design: the reader speaks from ScriptManager.msgBuf,
-- which holds the COMPLETE message before a single glyph is drawn, so speech never waited for the
-- drawing in the first place. The only thing draw speed changes is how long before A will turn the
-- page — and at the game's fastest that is about a second for a two-line box, which he measured as
-- fine. Patching the game's code to save that second was not a trade worth making.
--
-- The whole investigation is kept in team/report-20260904-pt-text-speed.md, including the four
-- verified patch sites, in case a future screen genuinely needs it. Nothing here writes code.
local FAST_TEXT = true

-- Hotkeys. Only R C U L N are verified safe on Ola's binds (CLAUDE.md: F = Frame-Advance,
-- P = Load-State and S = a game button all collide with the emulator or the game).
local KEY_STOP   = "R"                 -- stop whatever is being said
local KEY_REPEAT = "U"                 -- say the last line again

-------------------------------------------------------------- CORE HELPERS ---
-- Reading the console's RAM is the same job in every game, so it lives in core/mem.lua. The names
-- are pulled out as locals because they are called thousands of times and a table lookup per read
-- would be the one place in this file where that actually matters.
--
-- ⛔ inram IS NIL-TOLERANT ON PURPOSE. A nil address is self-evidently not a RAM address, and
-- answering false is the fail-closed answer every caller already expects. A raised Lua error does
-- not silence one reader — it kills the whole SCRIPT, and a blind player then loses every bit of
-- speech mid-session with no way to tell that from a quiet moment in the game (Rule 10).
local mem = core("mem")
local inram, u8, u16, u32, w8 = mem.inram, mem.u8, mem.u16, mem.u32, mem.w8
-- s32 for the overworld only: Gen IV keeps tile coordinates as signed `int`, and reading one unsigned
-- turns a negative into ~4.29 billion — which poisons every distance and bearing computed from it.
local s32 = mem.s32

------------------------------------------------------------------- ROOTS -----
