	-- sources and the CARTRIDGE's own message charcodes; `--audit` prints every name and its slot.
	--
	-- ⭐⭐ WHY THIS ALONE ANSWERS "WHAT WOULD THE GAME CALL HIM RIGHT NOW". Platinum expresses story
	-- progress by SWAPPING WHICH OBJECT IS STANDING THERE, not by branching a name inside one script:
	-- Looker's "Mystery man" and his "Looker" are different ENTRIES on different objects, gated by the
	-- flags that hide and show them. So reading the LIVE object's scriptID gets the right name at the
	-- right point in the story without evaluating anything. Measured: of 4,079 script entries, 271
	-- reach a named speaker and only 18 name different people on different paths — and thirteen of
	-- those eighteen are Dawn-vs-Lucas, which is the player's GENDER, not the story.
	-- ⛔ ENTRY SLOTS ARE 1-BASED, and this is the Gen V trap that cost a game-wide off-by-one there
	-- (a plain Man on Route 1 announced itself as "Cheren"). Settled here by MEASUREMENT, not by two
	-- examples: over all 1,485 static object events, reading scriptID as 1-based makes the entry's own
	-- label agree with the object's own name 517 times, against 8 for 0-based.
	-- ⛔ AND THE FIRST MESSAGE ONLY — no skipping past an un-prefixed opener to find a prefixed line
